Trusted Platform Module  (TPM/HSM)

What TPM Means

Trusted → ensures the system is secure and verified

Platform → refers to the device (computer, server, IoT device)

Module → a hardware chip/module on the motherboard

So TPM is a security chip that stores cryptographic keys securely inside hardware.

------------------------------

What HSM Means

Hardware → a physical device

Security → protects cryptographic operations and keys

Module → a specialized component used for encryption tasks
---------------------
1. Hardware Security Module (HSM)

Hardware Security Module is usually a separate dedicated hardware device used to store and manage cryptographic keys.

It can exist in several forms.

Network HSM (most common in data centers)
Application servers
       │
       ▼
   Network HSM
       │
       ▼
 cryptographic operations

This device sits in a secure rack in a data center.

Used by:

banks

payment systems

certificate authorities

Example hardware vendors:

Thales

SafeNet

Utimaco

PCIe HSM Card

Some HSMs are PCIe cards inserted into a server.

Example:

Server
 ├ CPU
 ├ RAM
 └ HSM PCI card
Cloud HSM

Cloud providers also offer HSM services like:

AWS CloudHSM

Here AWS manages the physical hardware.

2. TPM (Different from HSM)

A Trusted Platform Module is usually embedded on the motherboard of the device.

Example in your tractor:

Intel NUC motherboard
     │
     └ TPM chip

So TPM is built into the device, not external.

3. Quick Comparison
Feature	TPM	HSM
Location	motherboard chip	external device or server module
Purpose	device identity	enterprise key management
Used in	laptops, IoT devices	banks, PKI infrastructure
Example device	Intel NUC	data center appliance
4. In Your Tractor System

For your fleet:

Tractor

Intel NUC
   │
   ├ TPM chip (hardware identity)
   │
   └ Docker + ROS + device agent

You would normally use TPM, not an HSM.

HSM would usually exist in your cloud infrastructure, for example to manage certificates.

✅ Simple summary

TPM → small chip inside the device

HSM → larger dedicated security device (often external or cloud)

----------------------

What TPM Does

A TPM chip provides:

Secure key storage
Device identity
Encryption support
Secure boot validation
Hardware-based authentication

Instead of storing private keys in files like:

/etc/certs/private.key

the key is stored inside the TPM chip, where it cannot be extracted.

Example in an IoT Device

Example on a tractor edge computer:

Intel NUC
   │
   ├ TPM chip
   │
   ▼
Private key stored in TPM
   │
   ▼
TLS authentication
   │
   ▼
AWS IoT Core

This improves security because the private key never leaves the hardware.
